# MobX coding style

Narrows the React/TypeScript common rules to MobX's own idiom for stores,
observables, and observer components. Everything not MobX-specific still
comes from the common and React rules this file `extends`.

## Store class shape

- Call `makeObservable(this)` (with an explicit annotations map, or
  `makeAutoObservable(this)` for a store with no inheritance and no need to
  keep some fields non-observable) once, in the constructor, before any
  other constructor logic reads `this`.
- Prefer explicit `@observable`/`@computed`/`@action`/`@action.bound`
  decorators with `makeObservable` over `makeAutoObservable` for any store
  a component subclasses, that mixes injected dependencies with state, or
  that needs a field to stay non-observable — `makeAutoObservable` makes
  every own enumerable property observable and every method an action,
  which is the wrong default once the store has structure to protect.
- Name store classes `XyzStore` and files `xyz.store.ts`/`XyzStore.ts` to
  match the project's existing convention; keep one store per file.

## Actions

- Every method that mutates observable state must be an `action`
  (`@action` or `@action.bound`). A public method invoked from a component
  event handler must be `@action.bound` so it can be passed as a callback
  reference without losing `this`.
- After an `await` inside an async action, re-enter MobX with
  `runInAction(() => { ... })` before mutating state — the code after the
  `await` runs outside the original action's transaction.
- Do not call a store method that mutates state directly from a component
  render body; only from an event handler, effect, or another action.

## Computed values

- Any value derived purely from other observables belongs in a
  `@computed get` accessor, not in a component-side calculation repeated
  on every render or a store method that recomputes and returns a fresh
  value each call. A `@computed` is cached and only re-evaluates when one
  of its own observable dependencies changes.
- Keep a `@computed` getter free of side effects; it must be safe to
  evaluate speculatively (MobX may read a computed's value to decide
  whether it changed).

## Observable collections and value types

- Use `@observable.ref` for a value the store treats as an opaque
  reference (a large externally-owned object, a DOM/editor instance) it
  does not want deeply proxied, `@observable.shallow` for a container
  whose items should stay plain, and `@observable.struct` when a reaction
  should only fire on a structural (deep-equal) change, not fine-grained.
  Reserve the default deep `@observable` for state the store actually
  wants MobX to track recursively.
- When you need MobX's own collection API (`.replace()`, `.remove()`,
  `.merge()`, `.has()`) on an array or map field, construct it with
  `observable([...])` / `observable(new Map())` and type it as
  `IObservableArray<T>` / `ObservableMap<K, V>`; a plain array/object
  literal assigned to `@observable` is still deeply observed but does not
  expose those methods.

## Components

- Wrap any function component that reads observable state in `observer`
  from `mobx-react-lite`. A component that reads a store field but is not
  wrapped in `observer` will render once and silently never update again
  when that field changes.
- Read store instances through a typed React context + hook pair
  (`useXyzStore()`), not through a module-level singleton import, so
  components stay testable and providers can swap the instance.
- Do not destructure observable fields off a store at the top of a
  component body if that breaks fine-grained tracking for a nested
  `<Observer>` boundary further down — read the field where it is
  actually rendered instead.

## Errors and imports

- Import MobX identifiers (`observable`, `action`, `computed`, `reaction`,
  `runInAction`, `makeObservable`) from `"mobx"`, and `observer`,
  `useLocalObservable` from `"mobx-react-lite"` — do not import from the
  deprecated combined `mobx-react` package in new code unless the project
  has not migrated off it yet.
- Catch and store errors from an async action's `try/catch` as
  `err: unknown`, narrowing before assigning to an observable `error`
  field (see `rules/patterns.mdc` for the full async-action shape).
